---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Composition recipes"
  description="A lot of UI screens aren't single images — they're assemblies of sprite chips placed at specific positions. The placement instructions live in the game's code overlays."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: '2D Art', href: `${base}/field-guide/2d-art/` },
    { label: 'Composition recipes' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    When you look at the shop's "buy item" screen, you might think it's a
    full-screen background image. It isn't. It's six or seven sprite chips
    layered over a tiled background, with each chip placed at a coordinate
    decided by the game.
  </p>

  <p>
    The placement isn't decided dynamically. For most static screens, it's
    a baked-in table in one of the game's code overlays. Once you find the
    table, you have the recipe for rendering the screen exactly as the
    game does.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example: the buyitem screen</h2>
  <p>
    The shop's buy-item screen has been traced end-to-end. Here's the path
    the game takes when you walk up to a shop counter:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>The state machine decides to show the buy-item screen.</li>
    <li>The "controller" code overlay <strong>ov003</strong> loads.</li>
    <li>ov003 calls the file loader to bring
      <code>2d/buyitem.lzs</code> and <code>2d/itemorderwnd.lzs</code>
      into the content cache.</li>
    <li>ov003 loads the "body" code overlay <strong>ov070</strong>,
      which is the actual renderer.</li>
    <li>ov070's init function reads its 10-row composition table and
      places sprites accordingly.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Each row in the table is 12 bytes — six 16-bit fields:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>cell ID</td><td>Which sprite chip to draw.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>palette / style</td><td>Which palette bank to use.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>sub-index</td><td>Secondary index for compound widgets.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>flags</td><td>Render mode flags.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>screen X</td><td>X coordinate on the 256-pixel-wide screen.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>screen Y</td><td>Y coordinate on the 192-pixel-tall screen.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The actual table for the buyitem screen:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Slot</th><th>Cell</th><th>Pal</th><th>Sub</th><th>Flags</th><th>X</th><th>Y</th><th>Role</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0</td><td>6</td><td>9</td><td>21</td><td>0</td><td>10</td><td>92</td><td>Left-side icon</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>9</td><td>21</td><td>0</td><td>220</td><td>92</td><td>Right-side icon</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>7</td><td>9</td><td>22</td><td>0</td><td>128</td><td>107</td><td>Center icon</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>9</td><td>18</td><td>0</td><td>125</td><td>100</td><td>Center near-button</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>19</td><td>24</td><td>128</td><td>96</td><td>Main center</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>5</td><td>13</td><td>20</td><td>25</td><td>128</td><td>96</td><td>Main center alt</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>10</td><td>15</td><td>0</td><td>128</td><td>96</td><td>BG center</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>8</td><td>14</td><td>23</td><td>27</td><td>128</td><td>96</td><td>Effect center</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>16</td><td>0</td><td>128</td><td>96</td><td>UI alt 1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>2</td><td>11</td><td>17</td><td>0</td><td>128</td><td>96</td><td>UI alt 2</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    The 11th row is all zeros — the terminator. Most screens have around
    this many entries.
  </p>

  <h2>The general pattern</h2>
  <p>
    Most 2D UI screens follow the same shape:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A "controller" overlay handles the state machine for the screen
      (what does the player see, what input is valid, when do we leave).</li>
    <li>A "body" overlay is loaded into a shared slot when the screen is
      active. The body's init function walks the placement table.</li>
    <li>The body's table is always 12 bytes per row, terminated by a
      zero row.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    We catalogued 35+ containers with their composed-screen renders. For
    most of them we know which controller overlay loads them, but the
    body overlay (and therefore the placement table) isn't yet known
    for every screen. The chest, dustbox, fukidashi, signboard, and
    several others are still in this state.
  </p>

  <h2>Why some screens can't be captured this way</h2>
  <p>
    Screens that resize, scroll, or animate based on player input don't
    have a static recipe — the placement is decided by code at runtime.
    The chat window, the fashion-design preview, and the mailorder catalog
    are all in this category. We have all the individual sprite chips
    extracted, but to capture those screens exactly as they appear in the
    game, we'd need to record the game in an emulator and grab the OAM
    state at the right frame.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
